      "id": 1,
      "text": "In 1608, Jamestown settler John Smith named the Patuxent River 'Pawtuxunt' on his detailed map of the area now North Laurel.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Laurel%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In 1890, a syndicate bought portions of local farms beside the B&O track to create a town called 'North Laurel,' but it never developed.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Laurel%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Ernest Lyon opened the Maryland Industrial and Agricultural Institute for Colored Youths near the Patuxent River in 1901, offering education during segregation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_Laurel%2C_Maryland",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Poor soil conservation in the area forced farmers to switch crops or abandon their farms altogether.",
